## PHAETHON1 - example tmesh commands to create a Phaethon 1
## machine:

##
## Despite being based on a CPU with a 24-bit address bus,
## The Phaethon 1 has 4 basic address space ranges, each
## 64MB in size:
##
## $000.0000 - $3FF.FFFF -- RAM
##                          (on-board and expansion RAM,
##                          fully-decoded)
## $400.0000 - $7FF.FFFF -- ROM
##                          (1MB, repeated over entire range)
## $800.0000 - $BFF.FFFF -- on-board I/O devices
##                          (only lower 12-bits decoded)
## $C00.0000 - $FFF.FFFF -- A24D16 VME I/O expansion
##                          (lower 24 bits forwarded to VME)
##
## When the MMU is disabled, all {User,Super}-{Data,Prog} bus
## cycles go to the ROM, which must then bootstrap the MMU
## before access to RAM is possible.
##
mainbus0: tme/machine/phaethon1
cpu0 at mainbus0: tme/ic/m68010
obmem0 at mainbus0 ram: tme/generic/bus size 64MB
ram0 at obmem0 addr 0x0: tme/host/posix/memory ram 8MB
obmem1 at mainbus0 rom: tme/generic/bus size 1MB
rom0 at obmem1 addr 0x0: tme/host/posix/memory rom pg68010mk1.bin
obio0 at mainbus0 obio: tme/generic/bus size 64MB
vme0 at mainbus0 vme: tme/generic/bus size 64MB 

## The on-board I/O devices:
com0 at obio0 addr 0x0000 ipl 5: tme/machine/phaethon1/com
com1 at obio0 addr 0x0100 ipl 5: tme/machine/phaethon1/com

## Console device is on UART 0:
##
# uncomment the following line if you aren't using the GTK-based
# display console above.  this will connect ttya to the master side of
# a pseudo-tty, which you can connect to using tip or some other serial
# communications program on the slave (in this case, a pty allocated
# automatically):
#
console0 at com0: tme/host/posix/serial device pty

## power up the machine:
##
# uncomment this line to automatically power up the machine when
# tmesh starts:
#
# command mainbus0 power up
